---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

/* Sourced from docs/COMPARISON.md, which is itself sourced from a survey of
 * Restreamer's issue trackers. The losing rows are kept. A comparison page that
 * only lists wins is read as marketing and discounted entirely; this audience
 * checks. */

const wins = [
  ["Per-destination audio mix from one multitrack ingest", "No", "No"],
  ["Channel-level mix matrix with per-cell gain", "No", "No"],
  ["Per-destination loudness target, measured after routing", "No", "No"],
  ["Multitrack archive — every ingest track preserved, stream-copied", "No", "No"],
  ["Per-track stems as 24-bit WAV or FLAC, segment-aligned", "No", "No"],
  ["Track annotations — what each incoming track actually is", "No", "No"],
  ["Typed SRT rejections — the publisher is told why it was refused", "No", "n/a"],
];

const losses = [
  [
    "WebRTC / WHIP output",
    "Not built. It is a sizeable subsystem and the real use case is sub-second self-monitoring, which the preview path does not try to be. Restreamer has it.",
  ],
  [
    "Maturity and deployment count",
    "Restreamer is years older and far more widely run, and that is not something a newer tool can talk its way out of. What polyemesis offers instead is a small surface you can read: one binary, one config file, and acceptance suites that publish real streams.",
  ],
  [
    "Hosted option",
    "There isn't one, by design. restream.io will run it for you and charge per destination; polyemesis is a binary you operate on hardware you control, with no per-destination pricing and nothing leaving your network.",
  ],
  [
    "Multi-user and roles",
    "One admin, deliberately — no permission model to configure, and none to get wrong. If you need per-destination permissions or team accounts, this is not the tool.",
  ],
];
---

<Base
  title="polyemesis vs Restreamer vs restream.io — self-hosted restreaming compared"
  description="A self-hosted Restreamer and restream.io alternative, compared honestly — including where polyemesis loses. Per-destination audio is the thing neither does."
  path="/comparison"
>
  <section class="wrap py-16 sm:py-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">Comparison</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-balance text-4xl font-bold leading-tight sm:text-5xl">
      Including the parts where it loses.
    </h1>

    {/* Primary, not amber. This is the most important paragraph on the site —
        it tells a reader to go use a competitor — but it carries no signal, and
        amber is reserved for things that do. */}
    <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl rounded-token border-l-2 border-primary bg-surface/60 py-4 pl-5 pr-4">
      <p class="text-pretty leading-relaxed text-fg">
        <strong>polyemesis exists for one case:</strong> <em>this</em> platform
        gets the clean mix, <em>that</em> one gets the full mix — from one
        upload and one video encode, with the picture never re-encoded to do it.
        Nothing else here does that.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        If that is not your problem, use Restreamer. It is more mature, more
        widely deployed, and it does the common restreaming job well. Saying so
        costs nothing: the people who need per-destination audio know who they
        are, and the people who do not are better served elsewhere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap pb-16" data-reveal>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">What polyemesis has that neither has</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-token border border-line">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[40rem] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-surface">
          <tr class="border-b border-line">
            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Capability</th>
            <th scope="col" class="w-32 px-4 py-3 text-center font-medium">Restreamer</th>
            <th scope="col" class="w-32 px-4 py-3 text-center font-medium">restream.io</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            wins.map(([cap, a, b]) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-line/60 last:border-0">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-fg">{cap}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center font-mono text-xs text-subtle">{a}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-center font-mono text-xs text-subtle">{b}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-subtle">
      The multitrack ask is not hypothetical. “Multiple audio tracks for Twitch”
      is an open request on Restreamer's own tracker — users asking it for the
      thing polyemesis was built to be.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">Where Restreamer is the better answer</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Ranked roughly by how often it gets asked for, which is the honest
        ordering. If one of these is your requirement, take it seriously — a
        tool that claims everything is worth less than one that tells you when
        to use something else.
      </p>
      <dl class="mt-8 max-w-3xl divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
        {
          losses.map(([t, d]) => (
            <div class="py-4">
              <dt class="text-[15px] font-medium text-fg">{t}</dt>
              <dd class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{d}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-subtle">
        The full version, with the issue-tracker evidence behind each row, is in
        <a
          href="https://github.com/rainmanjam/polyemesis/blob/main/docs/COMPARISON.md"
          rel="noopener"
          class="text-primary-bright hover:underline">docs/COMPARISON.md</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap py-20 text-center" data-reveal>
    <h2 class="text-balance text-3xl font-medium">Still the right problem?</h2>
    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
      <a href="/download" class="btn btn-primary">Install it</a>
      <a href="/#matrix" class="btn btn-ghost">Try the routing matrix</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
